```java
// Abstract class shares behavior AND owns instance state
abstract class Counter {
    private int count;                 // instance state
    protected Counter(int start) { this.count = start; }
    public void inc() { count++; }     // shared concrete behavior
    public abstract void report();     // subclass contract
}
```

*Interfaces with default methods share behavior, not state. When you need fields, constructors, or protected members, an abstract class is still the right tool.*

var orders = new ArrayList<Order>();
orders.add(new Order(1L, "Laptop", 1200.0, false));
orders.add(new Order(2L, "Mouse", 25.0, true));
orders.add(new Order(3L, "Keyboard", 80.0, false));
orders.add(new Order(4L, "Monitor", 300.0, true));

// forEach() - default method from Iterable
orders.forEach(order -> System.out.println(order.item()));

// removeIf() - default method from Collection
orders.removeIf(order -> order.shipped());

// stream() - default method from Collection
var total = orders.stream()
    .map(Order::price)
    .reduce(0.0, Double::sum);

// sort() - default method from List
orders.sort(Comparator.comparing(Order::price));

// replaceAll() - default method from List
orders.replaceAll(order -> 
    new Order(order.id(), order.item().toUpperCase(), 
              order.price(), order.shipped()));

record Order(Long id, String item, double price, boolean shipped) {}
